9. Newborn and Infant Care\
\uc0\u61623  Vaccines: First vaccine is typically Hep B.\
\uc0\u61623  PKU: Avoid milk, eggs, dairy, and meat.\
\uc0\u61623  Safe Sleep: Supine position, monitor for fever >100.4\'b0F, jaundice.\
\uc0\u61623  Circumcision Care: Apply Vaseline gauze to prevent sticking.\
\uc0\u61623  Congenital Heart Defects: Pulse ox screening.\
Newborn and Pediatric Study Guide\
1. Newborn Assessment and Reflexes\
\uc0\u61623  Temperature & Patency Check: Use rectal temp to check rectum patency; observe for\
imperforate anus.\
\uc0\u61623  Meconium: Should pass within 24 hours.\
\uc0\u61623  APGAR Score: Assess at 1 and 5 minutes; normal score around 9.\
\uc0\u61623  Common Findings:\
o Mongolian Spot: Bluish spot on back.\
o Vernix: White substance on skin at birth.\
o Moro Reflex: Startle reflex\'97arms and legs extend, then draw back with\
clenched fists.\
o Babinski Reflex: Toes hyperextend or dorsiflex when foot is stroked.\
\uc0\u61623  Head Findings:\
o Caput Succedaneum: Soft swelling on scalp, crosses suture lines.\
o Cephalhematoma: Blood collection between skull bones, does not cross suture\
lines.\
\uc0\u61623  Skin Color:\
o Acrocyanosis: Blue hands and feet, common in newborns.\
\uc0\u61623  Barlow Maneuver: Checks for hip dysplasia; assess hip stability and gluteal folds.\
\uc0\u61623  Feeding: Feed every 2-3 hours.\
2. Cold Stress & Respiratory Care\
\uc0\u61623  Cold Stress Effects: Hypoglycemia, respiratory distress.\
\uc0\u61623  Respiratory Distress Signs: Tachypnea, grunting, nasal flaring, retractions.\
\uc0\u61623  Interventions for Tetralogy of Fallot: If cyanosis is noted, place the infant in knee-chest\
position.\
\uc0\u61623  Surfactant: Fatty acid substance aiding lung function.\
3. Congenital Conditions and Pediatric Syndromes\
\uc0\u61623  Tetralogy of Fallot: Four defects (overriding aorta, right ventricular hypertrophy, VSD,\
pulmonary stenosis); signs include cyanosis, difficulty feeding.\
\uc0\u61623  Spina Bifida Cystica: Two types affecting the spinal cord.\
\uc0\u61623  Omphalocele: Abdominal wall defect.\
messages.downloaded_by\
lOMoARcPSD|5060599\
\uc0\u61623  Trisomy Conditions:\
o Trisomy 19 (Edwards Syndrome): Severe developmental issues.\
o Trisomy 21 (Down Syndrome): Single palmar crease, extra neck skin, slanted\
eyes.\
o Turner's Syndrome: Affects only females; distinctive physical features.\
\uc0\u61623  PKU: Missing enzyme for amino acid breakdown; requires testing within 72 hours of\
birth, managed with diet free from certain proteins.\
\uc0\u61623  Cystic Fibrosis: Thick lung mucus; treatment includes CPT, enzymes on food, high-calorie\
and high-protein diet.\
4. Nutritional & Developmental Care\
\uc0\u61623  Vitamin K Injection: Given to help blood clotting.\
\uc0\u61623  Special Diets:\
o Celiac Disease: Avoid gluten (wheat, rye, barley).\
o PKU: Diet excludes foods with high protein.\
\uc0\u61623  Feeding for Cleft Lip/Palate: Use special nipple, feed at an angle, keep upright postfeeding.\
5. Conditions and Interventions\
\uc0\u61623  Congenital Heart Defect Signs: Occiput prominence, small jaw/mouth.\
\uc0\u61623  Sickle Cell Disease: Prioritize pain management, oxygenation, and IV fluids.\
\uc0\u61623  Leukemia: Low platelet levels; bleeding precautions.\
\uc0\u61623  Muscular Dystrophy (Early Signs): Clumsiness, frequent falls.\
\uc0\u61623  Wilms Tumor: Kidney tumor; do not palpate abdomen.\
\uc0\u61623  Pyloric Stenosis: Projectile vomiting, RUQ olive-shaped mass; surgical treatment is\
pyloromyotomy.\
\uc0\u61623  Intussusception: Distended abdomen, jelly-like stools; barium enema is both diagnostic\
and therapeutic. Will have white chalky stools.\
Stage 1: Labor Phases\
1. Latent (Early Labor)\
o Cervix dilation: 1-4 cm\
o Contractions: 5-30 minutes apart, lasting 30-45 seconds\
o Mom\'92s Response: Mild discomfort, excited, interactive with family\
2. Active Phase\
o Cervix dilation: 4-7 cm\
o Contractions: 3-5 minutes apart, lasting 45-60 seconds (strong and long)\
o Duration: 4-8 hours, pain intensifies\
3. Transition Phase\
o Cervix dilation: 8-10 cm\
o Contractions: 2-3 minutes apart, lasting 60-90 seconds (intense)\
o Duration: 30 minutes-2 hours, very strong contractions\
