Stage 2: Delivery of Baby\
\uc0\u61623  Position: Place mom supine, partner and nurse assist with leg support during contractions\
\uc0\u61623  Monitor: Continuous Fetal Heart Rate (FHR) assessment\
\uc0\u61623  Presentation: Check presenting part (breech presentation requires C-section)\
Stage 3: Delivery of Placenta\
\uc0\u61623  Duration: From delivery of baby to delivery of placenta\
\uc0\u61623  Placenta Assessment:\
o Shiny/White (Schultze): Baby side of placenta\
o Dirty (Duncan): Mother\'92s side of placenta\
\uc0\u61623  Complications: Check for missing parts, especially on Duncan side; if parts are missing,\
manual removal may be required due to hemorrhage risk\
Stage 4: First Hour Postpartum\
\uc0\u61623  Hemorrhage Risk: Monitor mom for postpartum hemorrhage\
\uc0\u61623  Vital Signs: Every 5 minutes initially, then every 15 minutes\
\uc0\u61623  Fundus Assessment:\
o Should be firm and midline; if boggy, massage and ensure bladder is empty\
\uc0\u61623  Lochia (postpartum bleeding):\
o Lochia Rubra: Red, for first few days\
o Lochia Serosa: Pink, lasts 2-3 weeks\
o Lochia Alba: White/yellow\
\uc0\u61623  Comfort: Apply ice packs for pain relief\
Assisting with Delivery of Baby\
1. Preparation:\
o Set up the delivery room and instruments, covered with a sterile drape\
o Break down bed for mom\'92s positioning to push\
o Prepare meds: Pitocin, 2 bags of LR, Narcan\
o Check resuscitation equipment (O2, suction, ambu bag for mom and baby)\
o Turn on warmer, place towels and baby hat in the warmer\
2. Immediate Care After Baby\'92s Birth:\
o Suction: Suction mouth, then nose to clear fluids\
o Newborn Care:\
\uc0\u61607  Dry baby thoroughly, use bulb syringe as needed, and place baby with\
mom if stable\
\uc0\u61607  VS & APGAR at 1 and 5 minutes\
\uc0\u61607  Weight, measurements, and blood sugar check if mom is diabetic\
o Identification: Place armbands on baby, do footprints\
o Placenta Inspection: Save cord blood for lab tests if needed\
