9. E: Emotions\
o Common to experience postpartum blues for 3-5 days due to hormonal\
fluctuations:\
\uc0\u61607  Monitor for signs of postpartum depression, such as lack of eye contact or\
interaction with the baby\
Term\
\uc0\u61623  Involution: The process of reproductive organs returning to their prepregnant state.\
Head-to-Toe Newborn Assessment\
1. Initial Assessment\
o Vital Signs: Check respirations and heart rate for 1 minute first.\
o Overall Appearance: Observe color, symmetry, and respiratory effort.\
o Skin Assessment: Look for rashes, birthmarks, hemangiomas, milia, and check\
buttocks for Mongolian spots.\
2. Head\
o Fontanels: Posterior and anterior fontanels should be flat and soft.\
\uc0\u61607  Bulging: May indicate increased ICP (intracranial pressure).\
\uc0\u61607  Sunken: May indicate dehydration.\
o Cephalohematoma: Does not cross suture line; bleeding between skull and\
periosteum, resolving in weeks to months.\
o Caput Succedaneum: Crosses suture line; edema resolves in a few days.\
3. Eyes\
o Assess for symmetry and color.\
4. Nose\
o Check for patency and nasal flaring.\
5. Mouth\
o Inspect for cleft lip and palpate for cleft palate.\
o Assess sucking reflex.\
6. Ears\
o Check for symmetry; should be in line with the eyes.\
7. Neck\
o Assess range of motion (ROM).\
8. Clavicles\
o Check for fractures.\
messages.downloaded_by\
lOMoARcPSD|5060599\
9. Chest\
o Heart Rate and Lung Sounds: Assess over 1 full minute.\
o Lungs: Respiratory rate 30-60; may hear a few crackles (amniotic fluid).\
o Heart: Rate 120-160; listen for rate, rhythm, and murmurs.\
10. Circulation\
o Assess brachial and femoral pulses.\
o Measure blood pressure in upper and lower extremities.\
o Check capillary refill over the sternum.\
11. Abdomen\
o Inspect, auscultate, and palpate.\
o Cord: 2 arteries, 1 vein.\
12. Genitourinary\
o Female: Check for ambiguous genitalia; labia majora may be swollen with white\
discharge.\
o Male: Check penis for hypospadias; urinary meatus should be midline.\
\uc0\u61607  Scrotum should be pink and may be swollen; testes should be descended.\
13. Anus\
o Check for patency; take rectal temperature.\
o Meconium: Should be passed within the first 24 hours.\
14. Hips\
o Assess range of motion (ROM).\
o Barlow Maneuver: Adduct hips and apply posterior pressure; a "clunk" indicates\
dislocated femoral head.\
o Ortolani Maneuver: Abduct leg and rotate out to relocate dislocated hip; a\
"clunk" indicates hip relocation.\
15. Musculoskeletal\
o Assess tone and ROM of extremities.\
o Count fingers and toes.\
16. Back\
o Check spine; look for any dimpling in the skin.\
o Assess gluteal folds for symmetry (uneven folds may indicate hip problems).\
Breastfeeding Positions\
1. Laid-Back Nursing Position\
o Description: Relaxed position where the mother reclines slightly, allowing the\
baby to find the breast naturally.\
o Benefits: Encourages a good latch; ideal for any mother and baby.\
messages.downloaded_by\
lOMoARcPSD|5060599\
2. Cradle Hold\
o Description: The baby is cradled in the mother's arms, resting along the forearm.\
o Benefits: Comfortable once the baby has latched on; suitable for many mothers.\
3. Cross-Cradle Hold\
o Description: The mother uses the opposite arm to support the baby, allowing\
better control of the baby's head.\
o Benefits: Particularly helpful for premature infants, newborns, or babies who\
have difficulty latching.\
4. Football Hold\
o Description: The baby is held under the mother's arm, resembling holding a\
football.\
o Benefits: Great for nursing twins, beneficial for mothers recovering from a Csection,\
or those with large breasts or flat/inverted nipples.\
5. Side-Lying Position\
o Description: The mother lies on her side, with the baby positioned facing her.\
o Benefits: Excellent for nighttime feedings and recovery after a C-section.\
Breastfeeding Guide\
Colostrum:\
\uc0\u61623  Definition: The first pre-milk produced by the mother.\
\uc0\u61623  Importance: Rich in antibodies and immunoglobulins that strengthen the baby's\
immune system.\
Milk Transition:\
1. Transitional Milk:\
o Begins 2-3 days after birth, lasting about 2 weeks.\
2. Mature Milk:\
o Produced after the transitional phase.\
Breastfeeding Timing:\
\uc0\u61623  Optimal Start: Within the first hour of life when the baby is more alert.\
Positioning:\
\uc0\u61623  Skin-to-Skin: Position the mother skin-to-skin with the baby.\
\uc0\u61623  Latching: Stroke the baby's cheek with the nipple and ensure the whole areola is in the\
baby's mouth.\
messages.downloaded_by\
lOMoARcPSD|5060599\
Feeding Schedule:\
\uc0\u61623  Nurse every 2-3 hours around the clock.\
\uc0\u61623  Duration: 10-15 minutes on one side before switching.\
\uc0\u61623  Next feeding should start on the side finished during the previous session.\
Breaking Suction:\
\uc0\u61623  Teach the mother to break suction using her finger and to burp the baby after each\
breast.\
Indicators of Adequate Intake:\
\uc0\u61623  6-8 wet diapers per day.\
\uc0\u61623  Presence of let-down reflex.\
\uc0\u61623  Audible swallowing during feeding.\
\uc0\u61623  Steady weight gain.\
\uc0\u61623  Baby appears content after feedings.\
Jaundice in Newborns\
Definition:\
Jaundice is a yellowish discoloration of the skin and eyes in newborns caused by elevated levels\
of bilirubin.\
Bilirubin:\
\uc0\u61623  Description: A yellowish pigment produced during the breakdown of red blood cells.\
\uc0\u61623  Function: Normally processed by the liver and excreted from the body.\
Causes:\
\uc0\u61623  In utero, the mother removes bilirubin for the fetus. After birth, the newborn\'92s liver may\
not be mature enough to handle bilirubin elimination, leading to jaundice.\
Progression:\
\uc0\u61623  Mild Jaundice: Seen on the face and chest.\
\uc0\u61623  Severe Jaundice: Progresses to arms, trunk, and legs.\
Risk Factors:\
\uc0\u61623  Premature birth\
\uc0\u61623  Feeding difficulties\
\uc0\u61623  Ethnic backgrounds (East Asian and Mediterranean descent)\
messages.downloaded_by\
lOMoARcPSD|5060599\
\uc0\u61623  Presence of bruising\
\uc0\u61623  History of jaundice in siblings\
\uc0\u61623  Darker skin tones (as jaundice is harder to detect, check gums and lips)\
Complications:\
If untreated, severe jaundice can lead to:\
\uc0\u61623  Kernicterus (brain damage)\
\uc0\u61623  Cerebral palsy\
\uc0\u61623  Hearing loss\
Treatment:\
1. Phototherapy:\
o Undress the baby, leaving only the diaper on.\
o Place the baby under UV phototherapy with eye protection.\
2. Increase Milk Intake:\
o Encourages bilirubin elimination.\
3. Blood Exchange Transfusion:\
o Considered for severe cases.\
Monitoring:\
\uc0\u61623  Bilirubin levels peak at 3-5 days old, making follow-up with a pediatrician crucial after\
discharge to ensure proper management.\
Umbilical Cord Care Guide\
Care Instructions:\
\uc0\u61623  Diaper Placement: Fold the diaper under the umbilical cord to avoid covering it.\
\uc0\u61623  Cleanliness: Keep the area clean and dry.\
\uc0\u61623  Bathing: Do not submerge the baby in water; give sponge baths until the cord stump\
falls off.\
Fall Off Timeline:\
\uc0\u61623  The umbilical cord stump typically falls off within 10 days.\
Signs of a Healthy Umbilical Cord Stump:\
\uc0\u61623  The stump should appear dry and may have a small amount of yellowish discharge.\
\uc0\u61623  Avoid any signs of redness, swelling, or foul odor, which may indicate infection.\
messages.downloaded_by\
lOMoARcPSD|5060599\
Pulse Oximeter Application in Newborns\
Purpose:\
\uc0\u61623  To check oxygenation levels and help rule out critical congenital heart defects (CCHD).\
Placement:\
1. Right Hand: Place one pulse oximeter probe on the pinky side of the right hand.\
2. Foot: Place another probe on either foot, also on the pinky side.\
Application Tips:\
\uc0\u61623  Ensure the light emitter on the probe aligns with the photodetector.\
\uc0\u61623  Use a new probe for each patient to prevent cross-contamination.\
\uc0\u61623  Make sure there are no gaps between the sensor and the skin for accurate readings.\
\uc0\u61623  Keep the newborn warm, as movement and shivering can affect pulse oximetry\
readings.\
Monitoring:\
\uc0\u61623  Look for the pleth waveform, which should match the heart rate (check apical pulse).\
Applying a Urine Bag\
Preparation:\
1. Clean the area using three towelettes or soap and water, then rinse.\
For Females:\
\uc0\u61623  Clean the labia and meatus from front to back.\
For Males:\
\uc0\u61623  Clean the tip of the penis to the scrotum three times (do not forcefully retract the\
foreskin).\
Placement:\
\uc0\u61623  Females: Position the bag on the labia, adhering the creased part to the perineal area.\
\uc0\u61623  Males: Place the penis and scrotum in the bag, adhering the creased part to the perineal\
area.\
Securing the Bag:\
messages.downloaded_by\
lOMoARcPSD|5060599\
\uc0\u61623  Place a diaper over the bag to prevent it from being pulled off.\
Collecting Specimen:\
\uc0\u61623  If the bag has a tab, rip it to pour urine into a specimen cup. If not, seal the bag and\
place it in a specimen cup.\
Circumcision Care\
Gomco Method:\
\uc0\u61623  Apply petroleum gauze to the head of the penis to prevent sticking to the diaper.\
Plastibell Method:\
\uc0\u61623  Clean the penis with water after each void and bowel movement (squeeze water over\
the penis). The plastic ring prevents the penis from sticking to the diaper.\
Diaper Placement:\
\uc0\u61623  Apply a clean diaper snugly, folding it under the cord.\
Post-Operative Care:\
\uc0\u61623  Avoid placing the baby on their stomach for 3-5 days.\
\uc0\u61623  Monitor for signs of infection, bleeding, increased temperature, and urinary retention.\
Infant Identification Upon Delivery\
Bracelet Information:\
Ensure that each bracelet is filled out with the following details:\
1. Mother\'92s Name: Clearly print the full name of the mother.\
2. Sex of Infant: Indicate whether the infant is male or female.\
3. Doctor's Name: Include the name of the attending physician.\
4. Date of Birth (DOB): Write the infant's date of birth.\
5. Time of Birth: Note the exact time the infant was born.\
6. Medical Record Number (MR #): Include the hospital's medical record number for the\
infant\
Newborn Footprint Procedure\
Timing:\
messages.downloaded_by\
lOMoARcPSD|5060599\
\uc0\u61623  Complete the footprint 1-2 hours after birth.\
Materials Needed:\
\uc0\u61623  Ink pad\
\uc0\u61623  Paper form for footprints\
Procedure:\
1. Hold the Foot: Gently grasp the newborn's foot by the ankle.\
2. Ink the Foot: Press the foot onto the ink pad, ensuring all surfaces are well covered with\
ink.\
3. Make the Print: Firmly press the inked foot onto the designated area of the paper form\
in a heel-to-toe motion.\
