Newborn Screening Specimen Collection and Important Conditions\
Newborn Screening Overview\
\uc0\u61623  Purpose: A blood test performed before discharge to check for rare genetic, hormonerelated,\
and metabolic conditions that could lead to serious health issues.\
Key Conditions Screened:\
1. Phenylketonuria (PKU)\
o Definition: A metabolic disorder caused by a defect in the enzyme responsible\
for breaking down phenylalanine, an amino acid.\
o Signs/Symptoms:\
\uc0\u61607  Developmental delays and permanent intellectual disability if untreated.\
\uc0\u61607  Fair skin, light hair, and blue eyes due to melanin disruption.\
\uc0\u61607  May present with seizures, growth problems, behavioral issues, skin\
rashes, and a musty odor to breath, skin, or urine.\
o Treatment: Strict lifelong diet low in phenylalanine. Babies require phenyl-free\
formula, and older children and adults should avoid high-protein foods and\
aspartame.\
2. Galactosemia\
o Definition: A rare, hereditary disorder that impairs the body's ability to convert\
galactose (found in milk) into glucose due to a deficiency of the enzyme\
galactose-1-phosphate uridylyl transferase (GALT).\
o Signs/Symptoms:\
\uc0\u61607  Normal appearance at birth followed by poor feeding, vomiting, and\
jaundice within days or weeks.\
\uc0\u61607  May lead to hepatomegaly, growth failure, ascites, and diarrhea.\
messages.downloaded_by\
lOMoARcPSD|5060599\
\uc0\u61607  Risk of cataracts, liver failure, and intellectual disability if untreated.\
o Treatment: Early diagnosis and a strict lactose-restricted (dairy-free) diet are\
essential to prevent severe complications.\
3. Maple Syrup Urine Disease (MSUD)\
o Definition: A life-threatening metabolic disorder characterized by the inability to\
break down branched-chain amino acids (leucine, isoleucine, valine).\
o Signs/Symptoms:\
\uc0\u61607  Urine, sweat, or earwax that smells like maple syrup.\
\uc0\u61607  Poor feeding, vomiting, irritability, and lethargy within the first few days\
of life.\
\uc0\u61607  Can progress to seizures, convulsions, and coma without treatment.\
o Treatment: A diet very low in protein and BCAAs; regular medical management\
is crucial to prevent complications.\
4. Congenital Hypothyroidism\
o Definition: A severe deficiency of thyroid hormone present at birth, leading to\
impaired neurological function and growth.\
o Signs/Symptoms:\
\uc0\u61607  Lack of weight gain, fatigue, lethargy, and poor feeding.\
\uc0\u61607  Thickened facial features, abnormal bone growth, excessive sleep, and a\
large tongue.\
\uc0\u61607  Jaundice and low muscle tone may also be present.\
o Causes: Can result from a missing or underdeveloped thyroid gland, genetic\
defects, or maternal iodine deficiency during pregnancy.\
o Treatment: Routine screening for T4 and TSH levels. Treated with levothyroxine;\
early treatment within the first four weeks is vital to prevent permanent\
intellectual disability.\
Heel Stick Procedure for Blood Sample Collection\
1. Preparation:\
o Wash hands and don gloves.\
o Confirm patient identity.\
o Position the limb lower than the heart to enhance blood flow.\
2. Warm the Heel:\
o Apply heel warmers for 3 minutes to promote blood circulation.\
3. Clean the Heel:\
o Use an alcohol swab to cleanse the heel and allow it to dry.\
4. Perform the Heel Stick:\
o Make the puncture to collect blood for screening.\
