Antidepressants\
Rules for Antidepressants:\
1. Suicide Risk: Increased risk during the first few weeks, especially in young adults (18-24)\
and children.\
o Nursing Interventions:\
\uc0\u61607  Clarify new prescriptions and monitor for suicidal thoughts.\
\uc0\u61607  Observe for unusual behavior and worsening depression.\
\uc0\u61607  Watch for sudden mood changes (e.g., increased energy with improved\
mood).\
2. Onset: Slow onset (2-4 weeks); do not stop after 1 week. Taper off medications.\
3. Drug Interactions:\
o Avoid mixing SSRIs with St. John\'92s wort.\
o Avoid MAOIs with other antidepressants; a 2-week washout period is necessary.\
SSRI (Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors):\
\uc0\u61623  Mechanism: Inhibit serotonin reuptake, increasing serotonin levels.\
\uc0\u61623  Side Effects: Insomnia, weight gain, sexual dysfunction, seizures.\
\uc0\u61623  Serotonin Syndrome: Risk increased when mixed with certain drugs (St. John\'92s wort,\
MAOIs, tramadol).\
o Symptoms: Sweating, rigidity, increased heart rate.\
SNRI (Serotonin-Norepinephrine Reuptake Inhibitors):\
\uc0\u61623  Mechanism: Similar to SSRIs but also block norepinephrine effects.\
\uc0\u61623  Notable Drug: Duloxetine (Cymbalta) - treats depression and pain from fibromyalgia.\
TCA (Tricyclic Antidepressants):\
\uc0\u61623  Mechanism: Block serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake.\
\uc0\u61623  Adverse Effects: Dry mouth, urinary retention, cardiovascular issues.\
\uc0\u61623  TCA Toxicity Symptoms:\
o Tardive dyskinesia, akinesia, akathisia, pseudoparkinsonism.\
MAOI (Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors):\
\uc0\u61623  Mechanism: Increase norepinephrine, dopamine, and serotonin.\
\uc0\u61623  Notable Medications: Phenelzine, Selegiline, Isocarboxazid.\
\uc0\u61623  Acronym MAOOI:\
o M: Massive hypertension crisis risk.\
o A: Avoid tyramine-containing foods (e.g., aged cheeses, cured meats, fermented\
products).\
o O: Avoid certain OTC medications (NSAIDs, antacids).\
