Anxiolytics (Antianxiety Medications)\
Mechanism: Depresses CNS, increasing GABA for relaxation.\
1. Benzodiazepines (e.g., alprazolam, temazepam):\
o Uses: Anxiolytic, sedative, muscle relaxant.\
o Cautions: Highly addictive, use short-term, do not stop abruptly.\
o Overdose Antidote: Flumazenil.\
2. Barbiturates (e.g., pentobarbital, phenobarbital):\
o Uses: Short-term insomnia and anxiety treatment.\
o Risks: Increased toxicity due to longer duration in the body.\
3. Buspirone (Buspar):\
o Characteristics: Non-sedating, takes 2-4 weeks for full effect, no withdrawal\
symptoms.\
Antipsychotic Medications\
Mechanism: Affect dopamine receptors to reduce psychotic symptoms.\
1. Typical Antipsychotics (e.g., haloperidol, chlorpromazine):\
o Uses: Treat positive symptoms of schizophrenia (hallucinations, delusions).\
2. Atypical Antipsychotics (e.g., aripiprazole, clozapine, quetiapine, risperidone):\
o Uses: Treat negative symptoms (apathy, blunted affect, anhedonia, avolition).\
