\uc0\u61623  Allergic to immune globulin.\
\uc0\u61623  Hemolytic anemia.\
\uc0\u61623  Anemia.\
\uc0\u61623  History of coronary artery disease (CAD).\
\uc0\u61623  Bleeding disorders.\
\uc0\u61623  High triglycerides.\
\uc0\u61623  Kidney disease.\
\uc0\u61623  Diabetes.\
Nursing Points\
1. Confirm:\
o Mother is Rh-negative.\
o Baby is Rh-positive.\
o No allergies to immune globulin.\
2. Informed Consent: Explain the procedure to the patient and obtain consent if required.\
3. Administration:\
o Obtain RhoGam from the blood bank; check lot number and expiration date with\
another nurse.\
o Administer via IM injection into the ventrogluteal or vastus lateralis muscle.\
4. Monitoring:\
o Observe for respiratory difficulties.\
o Check vital signs (v/s).\
o Monitor urine output every 2-4 hours for 8 hours post-administration.\
5. Side Effects:\
o Possible fever, chills, back pain, unusual weakness, confusion, slurred speech, or\
changes in urine color (pink, red, or dark).\
6. Patient Education: Provide the mother with an ID card to inform future healthcare\
providers about her Rh-negative status and the RhoGam administration.\
Documentation\
\uc0\u61623  Document:\
o Lot number and expiration date.\
o Route of administration.\
o Dose given.\
o Patient education provided.\
